(backend)\n\nThe Vite dev server will serve the static files (HTML, JS, CSS) on its own port (default 5173).\n\nActually, Vite can serve both the SPA and proxy API requests. Let me configure it to proxy `/api` to the backend.\n\nFor SSE to work with Vite proxy, I might need to use a custom proxy configuration. Let me check...\n\nActually, the simplest solution is to have the frontend connect directly to the backend port. Since we have CORS enabled on the backend, this will work. The Vite proxy is mainly for static assets and API, but for SSE, direct connection is more reliable.\n\nLet me update the frontend to use direct connections to port 3000:\n\n```javascript\nconst API_BASE = 'http://localhost:3000';\n\nasync function loadMessages() {\n  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/messages`);\n  ...\n}\n\nconst eventSource = new EventSource(`${API_BASE}/api/stream`);\n...\n\nawait fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/messages`, {\n  method: 'POST',\n  ...\n});\n```\n\nThis is cleaner and more reliable for SSE.\n\nNow let me also think about the PGLite initialization. I need to make sure the data directory exists. PGLite should handle creating it, but let me be safe and use `fs.mkdirSync` to ensure the directory exists before starting.\n\nActually, PGLite's file:// backend should handle creating the directory. But to be safe, I'll add a check.\n\nLet me also consider the PGLite API more carefully. The `PGlite.create()` method returns a promise that resolves to a PGlite instance. The `dataDir` option can be:\n- `'file://./data'` for filesystem persistence\n- `'idb://my-database'` for IndexedDB\n- Or just a path string\n\nLet me use `'file://./data'` to be explicit about using the filesystem.\n\nActually, looking at the PGLite documentation more carefully, I see that the default `dataDir` is `'idb://pglite-data'`. To use filesystem persistence, I should use `'file://./data'` or just `'./data'`.\n\nLet me use `'./data'` for simplicity.\n\nNow, let me also think about error handling and edge cases:\n- What if the text is empty? We should validate on the backend.\n- What if the SSE connection is lost? The frontend should handle reconnection (EventSource does this automatically).\n- What about message ordering? We use `created_at` and `ORDER BY` to ensure correct ordering.\n\nLet me finalize the code and create all files.\n\nOne more thing: I need to make sure the server waits for the database to be initialized before starting to listen. I'll use an async function to handle this.\n\nAlso, I should handle the case where the server is restarted and the database already exists. The `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` handles this.\n\nLet me also consider adding a simple middleware or check to prevent empty messages.\n\nHere's my final plan for the server:\n\n```javascript\nimport express from 'express';\nimport cors from 'cors';\nimport { PGlite } from '@electric-sql/pglite';\nimport fs from 'fs';\nimport path from 'path';\n\nconst app = express();\napp.use(cors());\napp.use(express.json());\n\nlet pglite;\nconst clients = [];\n\nasync function initDB() {\n  // Ensure data directory exists\n  const dataDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'data');\n  fs.mkdirSync(dataDir, { recursive: true });\n  \n  pglite = await PGlite.create({\n    dataDir: `file://${dataDir}`\n  });\n  \n  await pglite.exec(`\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (\n      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n      text TEXT NOT NULL,\n      created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n    )\n  `);\n  \n  console.log('Database initialized');\n}\n\napp.get('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  try {\n    const result = await pglite.query('SELECT * FROM messages ORDER BY created_at ASC');\n    res.json(result.rows);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('Error fetching messages:', err);\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to fetch messages' });\n  }\n});\n\napp.post('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  try {\n    const { text } = req.body;\n    \n    if (!text || typeof text !== 'string') {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Text is required' });\n    }\n    \n    const result = await pglite.query(\n      'INSERT INTO messages (text) VALUES ($1) RETURNING *',\n      [text.trim()]\n    );\n    \n    const message = result.rows[0];\n    \n    // Broadcast to all SSE clients\n    const data = JSON.stringify(message);\n    clients.forEach(client => {\n      client.res.write(`data: ${data}\\n\\n`);\n    });\n    \n    res.json(message);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('Error posting message:', err);\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to post message' });\n  }\n});\n\napp.get('/api/stream', (req, res) => {\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');\n  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');\n  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');\n  res.flushHeaders();\n  \n  clients.push({ res });\n  \n  req.on('close', () => {\n    const index = clients.findIndex(c => c.res === res);\n    if (index !== -1) {\n      clients.splice(index, 1);\n    }\n  });\n});\n\nasync function start() {\n  await initDB();\n  app.listen(3000, () => {\n    console.log('Server running on http://localhost:3000');\n  });\n}\n\nstart();\n```\n\nWait, I should also consider that PGLite might have a different API. Let me check the exact method names...\n\nActually, I'm not 100% sure about the exact PGLite API. The `query` method should return an object with `rows`. The `exec` method is for executing SQL without returning results.\n\nLet me also consider that PGLite might use `PGlite.create()` or `new PGlite()`. The documentation says `PGlite.create()` is the recommended way.\n\nOK, I think my code is reasonable. Let me create the files now.\n\nActually, I just realized I should double-check the PGLite API. The `@electric-sql/pglite` package might have a slightly different API. Let me assume the following:\n\n```javascript\nimport { PGlite } from '@electric-sql/pglite';\n\nconst pglite = await PGlite.create({\n  dataDir: './data'\n});\n\n//